{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Protocol Data Unit.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Protocol Data Unit.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"protocol data unit protocol data unit","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Protocol Data Unit.md","fileName":"Protocol Data Unit.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Protocol Data Unit","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-22 23:34\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[OSI Layers Packets and Network Streams]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["OSI Layers Packets and Network Streams","hands-onnetworkprogrammingwithcandnetcore.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Protocol Data Unit.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Protocol Data Unit.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"protocol data unit protocol data unit","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Protocol Data Unit.md","fileName":"Protocol Data Unit.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Protocol Data Unit","heading":"Protocol Data Unit","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A protocol data unit is the data passed through a layer of the network stack together with the headers or footers that give that layer's peer enough context to interpret it. Encapsulation adds this context as data moves downward through a sender's stack.\n\nThe receiving host removes and interprets the corresponding layer information as the data moves upward. The exact name and fields vary by layer and protocol, but the purpose is to preserve the information required for standardized peer communication.","tags":["baby"],"links":["OSI Layers Packets and Network Streams","hands-onnetworkprogrammingwithcandnetcore.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Protocol Data Unit.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Protocol Data Unit.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none